\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Spatial distribution of the 397 charging stations across the Würzburg urban service area, with the central depot marked.\relax }}{3}{figure.caption.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Two initial plan strategies for the same set of stations. Left: routing-efficiency approach visits A second despite low urgency. Right: maintenance-urgency approach defers A to last, ensuring critical stations are covered first.\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Two replanning decisions under the same incoming disruption E. Left: dropping B (largest detour) leaves the most critical station unserviced. Right: dropping A (recently serviced, low power) allows B to be serviced and averts failure risk.\relax }}{7}{figure.caption.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Three-phase benchmark for the number of zones $k$. Top: Silhouette score. Middle: mean CFA total cost. Bottom: mean DB-Simple total cost. Each point represents the average over 100~simulation runs; colours indicate the benchmark phase.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Mean service day per zone for CFA under centrality-based (left) and value-based (right) zone selection, averaged over 500 runs. Green = serviced early, red = serviced late. Under centrality, the service order is strictly concentric: zones near the depot are always visited first, outer zones last. Value-based selection breaks this pattern -- economically critical zones are pulled forward to early service days regardless of geographic distance from the depot.\relax }}{39}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Total cost distributions for all four policies under centrality (light) and value-based (dark) zone selection (500 runs each). Boxes show interquartile range; whiskers extend to 1.5$\times $IQR.\relax }}{40}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Cumulative costs (top) and daily disruption counts (bottom) for Myopic and CFA under value-based zone selection, seed~497 (single run). The dashed vertical line marks day~9, where the gap widens most sharply. Myopic total: €\,52{,}491; CFA total: €\,37{,}191.\relax }}{41}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Distribution of paired cost differences (base minus rollout) per run for each policy, shown as histograms with 500\,€ bins. Positive values indicate the rollout lowered cost; negative values indicate the rollout increased cost. The dashed vertical line marks zero. The x-axis is clipped to $\pm $4{,}000\,€; a small number of extreme values (up to $\pm $6{,}260\,€) fall outside the plotted range. All variants use value-based zone selection (500 runs each). The annotation shows the share of runs in which the rollout produced a strictly lower cost.\relax }}{43}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Distribution of replan overrides per run for all four rollout variants (500 runs each). Dashed vertical line shows the mean.\relax }}{44}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Cohen's $d$ for all key comparisons. Dashed vertical lines mark conventional thresholds (small: 0.20, medium: 0.50, large: 0.80). Zone-selection effects are the only comparisons with a large effect size; all other comparisons fall below the medium threshold ($d = 0.50$).\relax }}{48}{figure.caption.14}%
